			"Message": "feat(Cocoon): Implement comprehensive health monitoring and configuration validation\n\nThis commit introduces robust reliability features to the Cocoon extension host, enhancing its operational resilience within the Land architecture.\n\nThe new `HealthService` provides real-time monitoring of critical Cocoon services (MountainClientService, IPCService, ExtensionHostService, etc.) with heartbeat tracking, performance metrics collection, and automatic recovery mechanisms. Built on Effect-TS, it employs declarative effects for side-effect management and integrates with Cocoon's service layer.\n\nAdditionally, the `ConfigurationService` now includes robust validation logic for configuration keys and values, preventing invalid data from propagating through the system. This ensures that configuration changes adhere to expected formats and ranges, improving data integrity.\n\nTogether, these changes significantly strengthen Cocoon's fault tolerance and monitoring capabilities, crucial for maintaining a stable extension host environment that reliably communicates with the Mountain backend via gRPC.",
			"SHA": "ab3c8c40bd758b07a4e4afde3b75866bbc9cad36"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-01-30T10:19:56Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				493,
				2
			],
			"Message": "feat(Cocoon): Implement advanced synchronization service and enhance configuration resilience\n\nThis commit introduces a comprehensive `SynchronizationService` in Cocoon, designed to handle real-time document synchronization with the Mountain backend via Tauri IPC. The service provides advanced features including conflict resolution strategies, performance monitoring, and robust error recovery with circuit breaker logic, aligning with Land's declarative effects-based architecture.\n\nThe `ConfigurationService` is updated to use new Tauri commands (`get_configuration_data`, `save_configuration_data`) for improved integration with Mountain, and adds conflict handling with exponential backoff retry logic. This ensures that configuration changes are reliably persisted even in the face of synchronization conflicts, enhancing the overall stability of the Cocoon extension host.\n\nTogether, these changes strengthen the core synchronization and configuration management capabilities of Cocoon, crucial for maintaining a consistent state between the extension host and the Mountain backend in the Land ecosystem.",
			"SHA": "02d6385f01a4aa396eb260f92423ebcbf6071a89"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
